<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kutná Hora</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kutná Hora is a town in Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, which grew rich from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines opened in the late 13th century. Around 1300 Wenceslaus II set up a royal mint there to strike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> groschen, and the town became one of the wealthiest in Bohemia, second only to Prague. Its Gothic church of St Barbara and the Cathedral of Our Lady at Sedlec form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kutna-hora.jpg" alt="Kutná Hora" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: al.trcka · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kutn%C3%A1_Hora_(003).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
